COMP/ELEC/MECH 450/550
Project 4 (2026)

What are we doing?
------------------

This project requires you to:
 1. Copy your isValidPoint, isValidCircle, and isValidSquare implementations
 from Project 2's collision_checking_2d/src/CollisionChecking.cpp into the
 supplied CollisionChecking.cpp, along with their helper routines and required
 includes. Keep the supplied Project 4 header.
 2. Implement Reachability-Guided RRT (RG-RRT) in "RG-RRT.cpp" and "RG-RRT.h".
 You should start from the existing implementation RRT in OMPL. Note that this
 time we are doing control planning, rather than geometric.
 3. Implement the exercise code that uses your planner in "Project4Pendulum.cpp"
 and "Project4Car.cpp".

Implement the planning exercises in C++. Your planning implementation must be
your own. You may add code to any of the supplied files. Python may be used for
visualization and analysis.

All code for this project should be placed under the `src` directory.

How do I run the code?
----------------------

Use the same environment as Projects 2 and 3:

    micromamba activate ompl_env
    cd project4_2026/template
    make

The Makefile uses the environment's Python and CMake to find OMPL and build with
C++17. Run make after each C++ edit. Two executables are produced:
 1. Project4Pendulum, which prompts you for either planning or benchmarking,
 what torque for the pendulum, and in the case of planning what planner to use,
 and
2. Project4Car, which prompts you for planning or benchmarking and in the case
of planning what planner to use.

Run ./Project4Pendulum or ./Project4Car from this directory. The starter compiles,
but the TODO functions do not perform planning until you implement them.

How do I check my code?
-----------------------

The accuracy of your implementation will be assessed. You are required to
visualize your paths generated in both cases for the report, and this
visualization will be an invaluable tool for your debugging process. You can
use Python to plot the pendulum in (theta, omega) phase space and the car's path
over the street obstacles. Export states with PathControl::asGeometric() and
PathGeometric::printAsMatrix(). Project 3's Panda-arm replay uses a different
path format; create plots for these systems.

How do I compare planners?
-------------------------

Implement benchmarkPendulum and benchmarkCar using ompl::tools::Benchmark.
Run at least 20 independent trials per planner for both systems, with torque 3
for the pendulum. Save the native OMPL benchmark logs, then convert them using
the tool in ompl_env:

    python "$(command -v ompl_benchmark_statistics.py)" -d benchmark.db *.log

Upload benchmark.db at https://planner-arena.kavrakilab.rice.edu/ using
"Change database" to compare results and download plots for the report.
Include your raw logs and report with the submission described in the handout.
Run make clean before zipping your source directory.
